cartridge "TREK/Andrew Beery/1984"

' TREK
' A ColecoVision game based on "Trek" by Andrew Beery, COMPUTE!'s Gazette
' October 1984 (Commodore 64, BASIC with machine-language routines).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: your starship has lost its energy crystals and limps along in
' a low orbit over a planetoid on auxiliary power. Gravity pulls it down;
' the stick's thrust raises or lowers the orbit. Drones fly in from the
' right and home in on the ship: each drone that hits costs 15 shield
' energy, and when the shields are gone the ship crashes. Now and then the
' plasma cannon on the surface fires a plasma ball that homes in too:
' shields are useless against it, one touch destroys the ship. While the
' ball is out the other drones stay away, and the ball's fuel lasts about
' two passes across the screen. Phasers (left button) destroy drones but
' cost energy and score nothing. Over the large mountain range in a low
' orbit, hold the right button to beam up energy crystals: the mountain
' flashes and the shields grow. Survive two orbits to win. The score is
' the time flown minus the direct hits; the best winning score is kept.
'
' From the original (orig/trek.bas, its machine code read by hand, run in
' jsc64): gravity pulling the ship down one pixel a game loop against
' faster thrust; three drones sliding left at speeds 1:2:3 (here 1, 1.5
' and 2 pixels a frame) and coming back at the right at the ship's
' height; the MOVE routine stepping every drone one pixel towards the
' ship, called 6/8/11/14 times a loop at levels 1-4 (here 6/8/11/14
' steps in 24 frames); a drone hit moves it off to the right and costs
' 15.1 energy (100, 85, ... 10, and the seventh hit is the crash);
' phasers along the ship's line moving a hit drone off to the right;
' the plasma ball rising from the ground, homing twice as often as a
' drone, its chance per loop 1% (levels 1-2) or 6% (3-4), 60 rounds of
' fuel, drones 0 and 2 switched off and the orbit clock stopped meanwhile;
' the beam-up only within 25 C64 pixels of the large mountain's sprite
' and below line 175 (low orbit), 2 hits taken back each time (+30
' energy, +2 points); the orbit as the ship's slow drift to the right
' (C64 x 80 to 150, then 40 to 150: an orbit and a half... here x 56 to
' 104, then 24 to 104); score = time minus hits; the high score only
' from a winning game.
'
' Additions and liberties: everything smooth at 60 frames a second (the
' C64 ran the rules in a BASIC loop of a few frames); the surface is a
' 512-pixel loop of tiles scrolling 1 pixel a frame (pre-shifted
' patterns; the C64 scrolled two mountain sprites); time counted in
' tenths of a second (C64 jiffies); one crystal every 16 frames the
' button is held (the C64 every loop) and shields at most 100 (no cap on
' the C64); a phaser shot costs 5 energy and 1 point (the C64 charged a
' whole hit, 15.1, for each loop the space bar was down) and passes
' through the plasma ball; the beam moves 12 pixels a frame; the ship
' glides back to the left at the start of the second orbit (the C64
' jumped); "CRYSTALS" shows on the HUD while a beam-up is possible (the
' mountain had no marker); levels chosen with the stick; no
' play-again question; plasma launch odds per frame 1/2048 (levels 1-2)
' and 5/2048 (3-4), the ball's fuel 300 frames.
'
' Controls: either controller. Stick up/down: thrust. Left button:
' phasers. Right button (held): beam up. On the title, up/down choose,
' left/right set the level, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, the title
' tune played once. `set sprite count` always given. Own xorshift random
' numbers. Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

